{"id":"privateca","corpus":"aws","title":"AWS Private Certificate Authority","sourceUrl":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/privateca/latest/userguide/PcaWelcome.html","license":"AWS Documentation © Amazon Web Services, Inc.","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"AWS Private Certificate Authority"},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS Private CA enables creation of private certificate authority (CA) hierarchies, including root and subordinate CAs, without the investment and maintenance costs of operating an on-premises CA. Your private CAs can issue end-entity X.509 certificates useful in scenarios including:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Creating encrypted TLS communication channels"},{"kind":"li","text":"Authenticating users, computers, API endpoints, and IoT devices"},{"kind":"li","text":"Cryptographically signing code"},{"kind":"li","text":"Implementing Online Certificate Status Protocol (OCSP) for obtaining certificate revocation status"},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS Private CA operations can be accessed from the AWS Management Console, using the AWS Private CA API, or using the AWS CLI."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Regional availability for AWS Private Certificate Authority"},{"kind":"p","text":"Like most AWS resources, private certificate authorities (CAs) are Regional resources. To use private CAs in more than one Region, you must create your CAs in those Regions. You cannot copy private CAs between Regions. Visit AWS Regions and Endpoints in the AWS General Reference or the AWS Region Table to see the Regional availability for AWS Private CA."},{"kind":"p","text":"ACM is currently available in some regions that AWS Private CA is not."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Services integrated with AWS Private Certificate Authority"},{"kind":"p","text":"If you use AWS Certificate Manager to request a private certificate, you can associate that certificate with any service that is integrated with ACM. This applies both to certificates chained to a AWS Private CA root and to certificates chained to an external root. For more information, see Integrated Services in the AWS Certificate Manager User Guide."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can also integrate private CAs into Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service to provide certificate issuance inside a Kubernetes cluster. For more information, see Secure Kubernetes with AWS Private Certificate Authority."},{"kind":"p","text":"Amazon Elastic Kubernetes Service is not an ACM integrated service."},{"kind":"p","text":"If you use the AWS Private CA API or AWS CLI to issue a certificate or to export a private certificate from ACM, you can install the certificate anywhere you want."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Supported cryptographic algorithms in AWS Private Certificate Authority"},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS Private CA supports the following cryptographic algorithms for private key generation and certificate signing."},{"kind":"p","text":"This list applies only to certificates issued directly by AWS Private CA through its console, API, or command line. When AWS Certificate Manager issues certificates using a CA from AWS Private CA, it supports some but not all of these algorithms. For more information, see Request a Private Certificate in the AWS Certificate Manager User Guide."},{"kind":"p","text":"For RSA or ECDSA, the specified signing algorithm family must match the key algorithm family of the CA's private key."},{"kind":"p","text":"For ML-DSA, the hash function is defined as part of the algorithm itself. There is no option to select a different hash function with ML-DSA. To maintain backward compatibility with the APIs, the same value is used for key algorithm and signing algorithm."},{"kind":"h2","text":"RFC 5280 compliance in AWS Private Certificate Authority"},{"kind":"p","text":"AWS Private CA does not enforce certain constraints defined in RFC 5280. The reverse situation is also true: Certain additional constraints appropriate to a private CA are enforced."},{"kind":"p","text":"Enforced"},{"kind":"li","text":"Not After date. In conformity with RFC 5280, AWS Private CA prevents the issuance of certificates bearing a Not After date later than the Not After date of the issuing CA's certificate."},{"kind":"li","text":"Basic constraints. AWS Private CA enforces basic constraints and path length in imported CA certificates. Basic constraints indicate whether or not the resource identified by the certificate is a CA and can issue certificates. CA certificates imported to AWS Private CA must include the basic constraints extension, and the extension must be marked critical. In addition to the critical flag, CA=true must be set. AWS Private CA enforces basic constraints by failing with a validation exception for the following reasons: Path length (pathLenConstraint) determines how many subordinate CAs may exist downstream from the imported CA certificate. AWS Private CA enforces path length by failing with a validation exception for the following reasons:"},{"kind":"li","text":"The extension is not included in the CA certificate."},{"kind":"li","text":"The extension is not marked critical."},{"kind":"li","text":"Importing a CA certificate would violate the path length constraint in the CA certificate or in any CA certificate in the chain."},{"kind":"li","text":"Issuing a certificate would violate a path length constraint."},{"kind":"li","text":"Name constraints indicate a name space within which all subject names in subsequent certificates in a certification path must be located. Restrictions apply to the subject distinguished name and subject alternative names."}],"lemmas":{"privates":["private","privates"],"enables":["enables","enable"],"enabled":["enabled","enable"],"enabling":["enabling","enable"],"creations":["creation","creations"],"hierarchies":["hierarchy","hierarchies"],"including":["including","include"],"includings":["includings","including"],"includes":["includes","include"],"roots":["root","roots"],"rooted":["rooted","root"],"rooting":["rooting","root"],"subordinates":["subordinate","subordinates"],"subordi